  "result": "I wrote the 30-minute lesson on context and memory (O3), and a short quiz on it is ready. I never got a clean word count. By my own section-by-section estimate it comes to roughly 4,100 words, near the 4,500 target but possibly a little short.\n\nThe lesson is `lessons/0001-context-and-memory-in-an-agent.html`. It is built around your makerspace assistant. By the end you can decide whether each piece of information goes in the context window, in a saved note, or in a manual the assistant searches. It covers:\n- **Context window:** what the model sees on one call, and context rot (recall gets worse as the context grows).\n- **Between steps:** the program resends the history on every call, so total cost is the sum of all the calls. It also covers trimming that history and summarising it.\n- **Between sessions:** nothing survives unless you save it, like a game save file. The training-record example comes from here.\n- **Retrieval:** pulling the right chunks from a long manual, plus a hybrid design for your 12 manuals.\n- **Notes as memory:** and a table of where each kind of information should live.\n- **Design review:** a comparison of two designs you can use to defend the choice.\n\nIt also has worked examples, practice checks, two exercises with the steps faded, and a warm-up on the agent loop. The main source is Anthropic's [Effective context engineering for AI agents](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents).\n\nOther files I wrote:\n- **Quiz:** `quizzes/0002.json` and `keys/0002.json`.\n- **Cheat sheet:** `cheatsheets/context-and-memory.html`.\n- **Resources and notes:** `RESOURCES.md`, plus an update to `NOTES.md`.\n\nAsk me for the quiz whenever you want to take it, or ask about any part of the lesson. I didn't pick a community for you to test your skills in, so that's open. Do you want me to look for a student agent-builder community, or is your design team enough for now?",
